x86_64-linux  One name, and it is the remainder every case with a constructor carries here: cxx1 emits `_ZN1ZC1Ev`, the complete-object form, beside the C2 that this file actually calls, where clang emits only the one it needs. An unused definition is not a wrong one - virtual-base-diamond.nonames records the same for the destructors. **The names this case is about all agree**, `_ZTI1Z` and `_ZTS1Z` among them, and its `__vmi` record is byte for byte clang's: `__vmi+16`, `_ZTS1Z`, flags 0, one base, `_ZTI1A`, `(8 << 8) | 2`.
x86_64-windows  Two names, and they are one gap seen from both sides - **the Microsoft RTTI for a base that is not at offset 0**. clang emits `??_R17?0A@EA@A@@8`, a base-class descriptor for A carrying that offset, and cxx1 emits `??_R4A@@6B@`, a complete-object locator for A - which A, having no virtual function of its own, has no use for. The same difference virtual-base-vbtable.nonames records for V. Nothing in this file reads either: an upcast to a non-polymorphic base is done at compile time on both compilers, a class-typed `throw` is refused for this target (throw-class.notarget), and `dynamic_cast` needs a polymorphic base. **The layout is what this case is about and cl agrees with it exactly** - vfptr 0, A 8, z 12, sizeof 16, measured with /d1reportSingleClassLayout - which is what the case's output checks here.
